  "Question": "Given \\(x\\gt0,\\) \\(y\\gt0,\\) \\(x\\gt y,\\) and \\(z\\ne0.\\) The inequality which is not always correct is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x+z\\gt y+z\\)",
    "B": "\\(x-z\\gt y-z\\)",
    "C": "\\(xz\\gt yz\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{x}{z^2}\\gt\\dfrac{y}{z^2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(xz^2\\gt yz^2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Adding or subtracting the same number preserves an inequality, so A and B always follow from \\(x>y\\). Also, \\(z\\ne0\\) implies \\(z^2>0\\). Multiplying or dividing by \\(z^2\\) therefore preserves the inequality, making D and E always correct.\n\nIn C, the multiplier is \\(z\\), whose sign is unspecified. If \\(z>0\\), then \\(xz>yz\\). If \\(z<0\\), multiplication reverses the direction and gives \\(xz<yz\\). For example, \\(x=2\\), \\(y=1\\), and \\(z=-1\\) satisfy every given condition, but \\(xz=-2<-1=yz\\).\n\nThus \\(xz>yz\\) is not always correct, choice C.",
